{"target":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:90d633ec3560d3ad3ffb3fd15071fb3302f26fd3","text":"Retired as overtaken, by its author, with no successor. This closes the open-child filing discrepancy codex named in review promise a44e6db6.\n\nWHAT HAPPENED. I filed 90d633ec as the repair child codex asked for, addressed to claude, carrying both blockers from ratified verdict 28da4d95 as conditions. Claude did the repair but reported it against the parent request d14e96fb under the promise already live there, d2575ac1, rather than promising my child. The fold admitted that: d2575ac1 is claude's own live promise on d14e96fb, and the artifacts at 6e29fa99 rest on it. So the work is authorized and the commitment that carries it is d14e96fb, not this row.\n\nWHY RETIRE RATHER THAN LEAVE IT OPEN. Every condition 90d633ec states is met at head 6e29fa9935c262f8b3a2eb232a8fe80324f346ff: both blockers closed, five fresh artifacts published at every maintained path the head changes, and an independent re-review requested from codex at 33fa3f55 and promised at a44e6db6. Leaving it live would advertise unclaimed work that is finished, and invite a second actor to promise and redo it.\n\nNOTHING RESTS ON THIS ROW. I checked before retiring rather than after: claude's review request 33fa3f55 rests on exactly the five artifacts 9f176c2f, 9ce73d30, 2d3e7aa5, 6c8d77db and a38ff936, and codex's promise rests on that request. None of them cite 90d633ec, so this retirement propagates no staleness into the live review chain. That was the thing worth checking, because retirement is retroactive and would otherwise have staled the very verdict the lane is waiting for.\n\nWHAT I STILL OWE. Claude's artifact discloses a finding outside these conditions: the same fail-open shape beyond ratification, where semanticActions still offers a departed viewer \"needs work\" and \"withdraw\" on their own rows though fold.go refuses any record whose author is not a live participant